Conclusion of the results.

| Scale | Resolution (Nx x Ny) | CPU Time (s) | GPU Time (s) | Speedup (x) |
|-------|----------------------|--------------|--------------|-------------|
| 1x    | 200 x 100            | 0.7867       | 0.5676       | 1.39x       |
| 4x    | 800 x 400            | 12.6326      | 0.6219       | 20.31x      |
| 8x    | 1600 x 800           | 63.4072      | 0.9621       | 65.90x      |
| 16x   | 3200 x 1600          | 234.1950     | 2.1090       | 111.05x     |


With the basic 200x100 grid, the GPU is not that much faster than the CPU—only about 1.4 times, 
this is due to the overhead of transferring data between the CPU and GPU memory spaces, 
which is more noticeable when the actual computation time is very short.
The time it takes to move the data from the computer's memory over to the GPU (the data overhead)
is almost as long as the calculation itself. 

When turning up the resolution (4x,8x,16x times), the CPU really started to slow down compared to the GPU.
Looking at the 16x scale, the CPU took almost 4 minutes compared to the GPU which only took 2 seconds. 
This shows, as the problem size increases the parallelism becomes more efficient. 
The CPU has to process cells more or less one by one, but the GPU can handle thousands of them at the same time, leading to that huge 111x speedup.